(() => {
'use strict'; const E=window.CANON_EXPAND;if(!E)throw Error('Expansion core missing'); const add=E.add;

add({unit:2,id:'shelf-skills',title:'The shelf, the timeline, and seven Bible skills',reading:'Luke 24:44–49',ref:[42,24,44,49],objective:'Use the seven original learning tracks to connect book knowledge with responsible interpretation.',body:[
'This course uses a 66-book Protestant shelf. Its nine navigation groups are Law/Pentateuch, Historical Books, Poetry/Wisdom, Major Prophets, Minor Prophets, Gospels/History, Pauline Letters, General Letters, and Prophecy. These are organizational labels, not nine pure literary genres: a single book can contain narrative, poetry, law, or vision.',
'The first three skill tracks build orientation. The Story traces ten beats against their books; Order practices groups, testaments, and all 66; Groups teaches boundaries and reading approaches. Luke’s reference to Law, Prophets, and Psalms reflects another way of speaking about Israel’s Scriptures, reminding readers that this app’s shelf is not the only arrangement.',
'Chronology separates shelf position, narrated events, and composition. Content adds summaries, casts, openings, authorship, and audiences. Dates and attributions vary in certainty, so a teaching timeline must not pretend every placement is settled. The integrated skill missions retain that practice while the curriculum marks those limits.',
'Themes traces fourteen threads across books, including covenant, wilderness, remnant, presence, worship, and mission. Verses connects passages with themes, speaker, recipient, and reconstructed wording. These skills serve contextual understanding; accurate recall by itself does not establish a sound interpretation.'
],simple:'Use book order to find a text, chronology to orient its setting, and content and context to understand it. They answer different questions.',vocab:[['Shelf order','The position of a book in this edition’s arrangement.'],['Narrative setting','The time and circumstances a passage depicts.'],['Composition','The writing and shaping of a text, which may occur later than its setting.']],deeper:'A shelf category can contain several genres. Likewise, a book’s narrated era and its date of composition need not coincide; some placements remain debated.',reflect:'Which of the seven skills would most improve your confidence as a new Bible reader?',model:'I might first learn the groups so I can find a passage, then use speaker and audience to avoid treating every sentence as directly addressed to me.',evidence:['Luke refers to Law, Prophets, and Psalms.','The scene connects understanding Scripture with a message and commission.','A book’s shelf position automatically identifies its date of composition.','Every book in one shelf group uses only one literary form.'],claim:'Organizing Scripture and interpreting Scripture are related but distinct tasks.',application:'Use shelf, setting, and textual context together when explaining a passage.',decisions:[['A learner assumes a later shelf position means a later event. What should they compare?','Shelf order, narrative setting, and composition separately.','Only the printed page number.','The three dimensions answer different questions.'],['Someone perfectly reconstructs a verse but misidentifies its speaker. What is still needed?','The speaker, recipient, and surrounding argument.','Only faster word recall.','Memory becomes useful interpretation when context is restored.']]});

add({unit:3,id:'garden-trust',title:'The garden: freedom, trust, and limits',reading:'Genesis 2:15–25',ref:[1,2,15,25],objective:'Distinguish humanity’s vocation, freedom, and dependence in the garden narrative.',body:[
'The garden is a place of provision and work. The human receives a task before the story of disobedience: cultivating and keeping what God has provided. Work itself is therefore not introduced here as a punishment. The setting holds receiving and responsibility together.',
'The permission to eat is broad, but it includes a boundary. In the story, freedom is exercised within a relationship of trust; it does not mean every possible action has the same consequence. The warning about death introduces the seriousness of that relationship without explaining every biological mechanism.',
'The account also identifies isolation as a problem. The arrival of another human is celebrated as shared humanity and relationship. The narrative’s language should be examined carefully before being made into a complete rule for every modern household or a ranking of human worth.',
'Read this scene before the rupture in chapter 3. Notice what can be lost: trust, shared life, access, and a sustaining home. Keeping that earlier goodness in view prevents an introduction to sin from making failure the whole definition of a person.'
],simple:'The story joins a gift, a responsibility, and a relationship. Freedom matters because our actions affect that relationship.',vocab:[['Vocation','A calling or responsibility within life.'],['Boundary','A limit that gives an action moral significance.'],['Dependence','Receiving life and resources we do not create for ourselves.']],deeper:'Distinguish a story’s explicit statements from a later theological explanation. The presence of a warning does not supply a detailed account of how human immortality would work.',reflect:'What changes when responsibility is understood as caring for a gift?',model:'I can treat a shared space as something entrusted to me. Caring for it is a response to its value, rather than a way to earn human worth.',evidence:['The human is assigned care of the garden.','Permission to eat includes a stated boundary.','The garden presents freedom as exemption from every consequence.','All work begins only after disobedience.'],claim:'Human freedom is pictured within provision and responsibility.',application:'Name a responsibility attached to a resource you share, and act on it.',decisions:[['A reader says all work is punishment. Where do you begin?','Locate the work assigned before Genesis 3.','Accept the claim because work can feel difficult.','Genesis 2:15 places cultivation and care before the rupture.'],['A group wants to define freedom from this scene. Which statement fits?','Choices are meaningful within relationship and limits.','A free choice cannot have consequences.','Permission and prohibition appear together; consequences do not make the choice meaningless.']]});

add({unit:3,id:'sin-mortality',title:'Rupture, mortality, and the tree of life',reading:'Genesis 3:8–24',ref:[1,3,8,24],objective:'Trace the consequences of sin while distinguishing mortality from inherited personal guilt.',body:[
'The human response to God now includes hiding and fear. When questioned, the humans direct attention toward others. The story portrays a rupture in trust and accountability before readers reach its account of toil and death. Sin is more than breaking an arbitrary rule; relationships are damaged.',
'The consequences extend through shared life: painful labor, strained relationships, and return to dust. These descriptions do not instruct readers to create suffering or preserve domination. Reporting a broken condition is different from commanding that condition as a moral ideal.',
'The expulsion closes access to the tree of life. This guide understands humanity as intended for enduring life with God, with mortality and a damaged condition inherited after sin. It does not treat inherited personal guilt as a settled conclusion or extend this claim to the absence of animal death.',
'God’s provision of clothing remains within the scene of judgment. Read judgment, consequence, and care together without pretending the rupture is trivial. Later Christian teaching connects this story with Christ’s saving work; that connection should preserve the details of Genesis rather than replace them.'
],simple:'The story explains damaged trust and lost access to life. Receiving a broken world is not identical to personally committing another person’s act.',vocab:[['Mortality','The condition of being subject to death.'],['Personal guilt','Responsibility for one’s own wrongdoing.'],['Consequence','An outcome of an action or condition, distinct from a command to reproduce it.']],deeper:'Compare the warning in Genesis 2 with the expulsion in Genesis 3. The narrative links life with God’s provision; it does not settle every later theory of original sin.',reflect:'How can you acknowledge an inherited problem without assigning identical blame to everyone affected?',model:'A community may inherit harmful practices. I should distinguish who caused them from who now has an opportunity to repair their effects.',evidence:['The humans hide and shift attention toward others.','Access to the tree of life is closed.','The passage commands readers to make relationships oppressive.','The passage explicitly states that no animal had ever died.'],claim:'The rupture has relational consequences and closes access to enduring life.',application:'Take responsibility for harm you can address without attributing another person’s act to everyone affected.',decisions:[['Someone calls a painful condition in Genesis a rule to enforce. What distinction helps?','Separate the described consequence from a moral command.','Treat every described condition as an instruction.','The narrative’s account of rupture is not a direction to create more harm.'],['A learner equates mortality with personally committing Adam’s act. How do you respond?','Distinguish inherited conditions from individual responsibility.','Use the words mortality and guilt interchangeably.','They name different questions, even where Christian traditions connect them.']]});

add({unit:3,id:'creation-care',title:'A living world entrusted to our care',reading:'Psalm 104:24–30',ref:[19,104,24,30],objective:'Read creation poetry and reason from creaturely dependence to responsible care.',body:[
'Psalm 104 praises the variety of living things. Its poetic attention moves beyond human usefulness to creatures with their own place in the world. Praise invites wonder before it becomes a list of tasks. The world has value that is not exhausted by what an individual can consume.',
'The creatures depend on provision and life-giving breath. The poem also names death and renewal. It uses theological poetry, not the language of a modern scientific model. Reading its claims about God does not require ignoring the natural processes through which living systems change.',
'Creation care is an application drawn from this value and dependence, in conversation with humanity’s responsibility in Genesis. The psalm does not specify a complete environmental policy. Moving from praise to policy requires additional evidence about consequences, resources, and the people most affected.',
'A manageable response begins with one shared resource: water, food, energy, or a neighborhood space. Investigate waste or harm, identify who bears the cost, and choose a realistic improvement. Responsible care involves learning whether an action helps, not simply feeling virtuous for having acted.'
],simple:'The poem celebrates a world full of dependent life. Care should follow that appreciation and be tested against real effects.',vocab:[['Praise','Speech that celebrates value and gives thanks.'],['Stewardship','Responsible care for what is entrusted to us.'],['Application','A reasoned response drawn from a text in a new setting.']],deeper:'A theological reason to care for creation does not identify the most effective policy by itself. Combine the moral purpose with reliable evidence about the proposed action.',reflect:'Which shared resource could you care for more responsibly this week?',model:'I could reduce avoidable food waste and check whether my plan actually saves food. The lesson supplies a reason to care, not a substitute for learning what works.',evidence:['The psalm celebrates many kinds of creatures.','It depicts living things as dependent on provision.','The psalm identifies one complete modern environmental policy.','A creature matters only when it is useful to its reader.'],claim:'Creation is valued as a community of dependent life.',application:'Investigate one shared resource and make a practical change that reduces harm.',decisions:[['A campaign quotes this psalm as proof its exact policy must work. What is missing?','Evidence about the policy’s actual effects.','A claim that poetry has no meaning.','The poem gives a theological concern; practical effectiveness requires further investigation.'],['Your first conservation idea burdens a vulnerable neighbor. What next?','Revise the plan with that person’s needs in view.','Keep it unchanged because the original intention was good.','Care includes the consequences of an action for neighbors as well as its intention.']]});

add({unit:4,id:'abraham-blessing',title:'Abraham: a promise that reaches beyond one family',reading:'Genesis 12:1–9',ref:[1,12,1,9],objective:'Identify the direction of the promise and distinguish calling from moral superiority.',body:[
'God calls Abram to leave a familiar setting for a destination yet to be shown. The story begins with initiative and promise, followed by a response. It introduces a particular family within a larger account of humanity rather than beginning with a claim that this family has earned every blessing.',
'The promise reaches beyond Abram. The language of blessing connects a particular calling with other families of the earth. Particularity and outward purpose belong together: the story follows real relationships, while refusing to make the gift simply private possession.',
'Abram travels and builds altars. These actions locate the response in places and practices, not only in inward agreement. Later episodes also expose failures and conflict. Being called does not make a biblical character’s every subsequent choice morally exemplary.',
'Read Christian applications with respect for the story’s place in Jewish Scripture and living Jewish interpretation. A modern reader may learn about trust and responsibility without claiming to replace Israel or treating a personal ambition as an identical divine promise.'
],simple:'Abram receives a calling whose blessing reaches outward. A chosen role is not proof that every action of its holder is right.',vocab:[['Calling','An invitation into a responsibility or relationship.'],['Blessing','Good received and, in this promise, directed beyond its first recipient.'],['Promise','An assurance whose speaker, audience, and setting matter.']],deeper:'Ask which parts of the promise are addressed to Abram and which broader concerns a later reader can responsibly apply. Similarity is not identity.',reflect:'How might a benefit you receive become good for someone beyond your immediate circle?',model:'A useful skill can become a way to help a neighbor. That is an application of outward responsibility, not a claim that Abram’s exact promise was made to me.',evidence:['God calls Abram to leave a familiar setting.','The promise connects Abram with blessing beyond his family.','The promise certifies every later act of Abram as right.','A reader may transfer every detail directly to a personal business plan.'],claim:'The calling connects a particular recipient with an outward purpose.',application:'Identify a way a benefit you have received can serve people beyond yourself.',decisions:[['A learner treats Abram’s calling as a certificate of moral perfection. What helps?','Distinguish receiving a role from always using it well.','Assume every action of a called person is exemplary.','A calling does not remove the need to evaluate later conduct.'],['Someone claims this guarantees a specific career outcome. What question comes first?','Who receives this promise in its narrative setting?','How strongly does the modern reader desire the outcome?','A personal application must account for the original recipient and scope.']],sequence:['Arrange the movement of Genesis 12:1–9.',['A call and promise are given.','Abram leaves with his household.','The journey reaches Canaan.','Abram builds altars as the journey continues.'],'Follow the narrative from speech to departure, arrival, and worship.']});

add({unit:4,id:'exodus-call',title:'Exodus: hearing suffering and answering a call',reading:'Exodus 3:7–15',ref:[2,3,7,15],objective:'Explain how the call of Moses connects divine concern with human participation.',body:[
'God’s speech begins with the suffering of the people: seeing, hearing, and knowing their affliction. Liberation is introduced through attention to oppression. The story gives the enslaved community’s experience a central place rather than describing their suffering as an inconvenience for rulers.',
'Moses is called into the response, and he asks questions about his role. Divine initiative and human participation are not presented as alternatives. A task can be significant while the person asked to undertake it remains uncertain and in need of support.',
'The assurance of divine presence answers Moses’ concern about adequacy. The passage also introduces God’s self-identification and connection with the ancestors. Treat the divine name reverently and attend to translation notes; one English rendering does not exhaust its linguistic or theological significance.',
'Exodus can shape a Christian concern for people under oppression without making every current conflict a one-to-one reenactment. Identify the specific harm, listen to those experiencing it, and consider responsible participation. Quoting liberation language cannot excuse ignoring evidence or the agency of those being helped.'
],simple:'The call starts by taking suffering seriously. Moses is invited to participate even while asking honest questions.',vocab:[['Oppression','The use of power to constrain or harm others.'],['Liberation','Release from a condition of bondage or domination.'],['Commission','A task entrusted to someone with a purpose.']],deeper:'Compare what God announces, what Moses asks, and what Moses is commissioned to do. This is a narrated encounter, not a formula promising success for every project.',reflect:'What would listening change in a plan intended to help someone?',model:'I should ask what the affected person needs before deciding on a solution. Concern becomes more responsible when their experience shapes my response.',evidence:['God’s speech attends to the people’s affliction.','Moses questions his adequacy for the task.','Uncertainty automatically disqualifies someone from participation.','Any project called liberation is guaranteed to succeed.'],claim:'Divine concern is joined to a call for human participation amid uncertainty.',application:'Listen to people experiencing a harm before deciding how to help.',decisions:[['A volunteer feels that asking questions proves unfaithfulness. What does Moses’ scene show?','Questions can occur within a serious response to a call.','A meaningful calling removes all uncertainty immediately.','Moses’ questions remain part of the encounter rather than being erased.'],['A group labels its plan an exodus without asking affected people. What should change?','Begin by attending to their actual experience.','Preserve the plan because the biblical label settles its value.','The passage begins with attention to suffering; an analogy does not validate a plan by itself.']]});

add({unit:4,id:'torah-neighbor',title:'Torah: holiness in everyday relationships',reading:'Leviticus 19:9–18',ref:[3,19,9,18],objective:'Connect covenant instruction with concrete care while avoiding mechanical transfer of ancient law.',body:[
'The selected instructions include farming, wages, truthful dealing, impartial judgment, and treatment of neighbors. Holiness is not limited to ritual activity. Economic decisions and ordinary speech belong within the community’s response to God.',
'The harvest instructions leave access to food for poor people and foreigners. They address a particular agricultural arrangement. A present-day reader must distinguish that arrangement from the concern for access and protection it expresses before choosing a modern response.',
'Commands concerning justice resist favoritism, whether motivated by poverty or power. The passage also links rebuke, resentment, and love of neighbor. Love is not simply a pleasant feeling; it can require honest speech and changes in conduct that make another person less vulnerable.',
'Christians disagree about the continuing application of particular Torah laws. This lesson practices a method rather than settling every disputed case: identify the original instruction, examine its purpose and setting, read it in the wider scriptural conversation, and explain the reasoning behind a contemporary application.'
],simple:'These laws place care and honesty inside everyday life. Applying them today requires understanding both the original arrangement and its concern.',vocab:[['Torah','Instruction, often referring to the first five books of the Bible.'],['Gleaning','Gathering produce left after the main harvest.'],['Impartiality','Refusing to distort judgment because of a person’s status.']],deeper:'An ancient law’s social setting helps explain its function. Explanation alone does not settle all moral questions or eliminate differences among Christian and Jewish readings.',reflect:'Where might an everyday financial decision expose another person to avoidable harm?',model:'Paying someone promptly can protect their ability to meet basic needs. I can connect that action to the passage without pretending my circumstances are an ancient harvest.',evidence:['Harvest instructions provide access for poor people and foreigners.','The passage addresses wages and honest judgment.','Holiness has no connection with economic conduct.','Every contemporary application must reproduce ancient farming arrangements exactly.'],claim:'Covenant responsibility reaches ordinary economic and social relationships.',application:'Choose a practice that improves access or honest dealing in your setting, and explain the connection.',decisions:[['An urban reader says gleaning has nothing to teach them. What is a better next step?','Ask what concern the original arrangement addresses.','Ignore the original arrangement and invent any meaning.','The ancient practice matters because its function helps discipline a later application.'],['A manager thinks generosity excuses withholding earned wages. What tension appears?','Care includes fair dealing, not only voluntary gifts.','One generous gesture cancels every other obligation.','The passage places obligations about wages beside other forms of care.']]});

add({unit:5,id:'land-judges',title:'Land and Judges: learning from a disturbing narrative',reading:'Judges 2:6–19',ref:[7,2,6,19],objective:'Trace the Judges cycle while refusing to equate a narrated act with a model to imitate.',body:[
'The passage looks back to Joshua’s generation and then describes a changed situation after it. Forgetting, wrongdoing, distress, and deliverance form a recurring pattern. The sequence gives beginners a way to locate the book between settlement and the monarchy.',
'Judges are portrayed as figures of deliverance, but the book does not present an uncomplicated gallery of moral heroes. Read each action within the narrative’s evaluation. A person’s role in a rescue does not make every action they take exemplary.',
'The larger land narratives contain conquest, attributed divine commands, and serious violence. Naming genre or ancient setting does not eliminate the moral difficulty. Distinguish what a narrator reports, what is attributed to God, and what a later interpreter argues should follow.',
'The guide evaluates present conduct through Jesus’ teaching, love, and human dignity; it does not authorize copying ancient violence. The Story lab connects this stage with the books carrying the arc, while Chronology distinguishes its narrative setting from the order of books on the shelf.'
],simple:'The cycle explains a pattern of crisis and rescue. It does not turn every participant into a moral example.',vocab:[['Judge','In this setting, a figure associated with leadership and deliverance.'],['Narrative cycle','A repeated pattern of events within a story.'],['Endorsement','Approval of an action, which must be distinguished from merely reporting it.']],deeper:'When a narrative attributes an act or command to God, name that feature directly instead of treating it as mere human description. The resulting moral question still deserves examination.',reflect:'How could you learn from a story without imitating its violence?',model:'I could examine the consequences of forgotten responsibility and failures of leadership while making clear that ancient conflict is not a rule for my conduct.',evidence:['The passage describes repeated distress and deliverance.','It connects the pattern with changes across generations.','Every action of every judge is automatically endorsed.','Ancient narrative gives readers unrestricted permission to imitate violence.'],claim:'The recurring cycle makes communal failure and deliverance visible without certifying every participant’s conduct.',application:'Evaluate a narrated action in context before proposing it as a present-day example.',decisions:[['A learner wants to imitate a violent act because a judge performed it. What question matters?','How the narrative presents and evaluates the act.','Only whether the actor appears among biblical leaders.','Role and endorsement are not identical.'],['A summary omits all troubling violence to make the book easier. What should change?','Name the difficulty and distinguish the relevant claims.','Keep only episodes that fit a reassuring picture.','A responsible introduction prepares readers for difficult material rather than hiding it.']],sequence:['Reconstruct the recurring pattern described in this passage.',['A generation turns from faithfulness.','The people experience distress.','Deliverance comes through raised-up judges.','The pattern returns after the judge’s death.'],'Follow the repeated movement rather than treating each rescue as the end of every problem.']});

add({unit:5,id:'kings-accountability',title:'Kingship: evaluating power rather than admiring it',reading:'1 Samuel 8:10–22',ref:[9,8,10,22],objective:'Identify the costs of royal power and distinguish biblical description from endorsement.',body:[
'Samuel’s warning repeatedly describes what a king will take. People, produce, and land become resources for royal purposes. The repetition directs attention to the costs of concentrated power rather than only to its promises of stability or prestige.',
'The people still ask for a king, including the desire to resemble other nations and have leadership in battle. The narrative lets competing desires remain visible. A request can be understandable while carrying consequences its supporters would prefer not to notice.',
'Later biblical accounts include hopes for righteous kings as well as severe criticism of rulers. This warning should be read within that tension. It does not turn every modern institution into a biblical equivalent, nor does a ruler’s appearance in Scripture certify their conduct.',
'Apply the reading by asking concrete questions about power: who benefits, who supplies the labor, who bears the loss, and how decisions can be challenged. Evaluating a leader requires more than admiration for strength or identification with a favored group.'
],simple:'The warning asks what power takes from people. Evaluate consequences as well as promises.',vocab:[['Monarchy','Government centered on a king or queen.'],['Accountability','The obligation to answer for decisions and their effects.'],['Description','An account of what occurs, which is not automatically approval.']],deeper:'Hold this warning beside later hopes for just rule. A biblical collection can preserve a real tension rather than supply one slogan for every political setting.',reflect:'Which question about power do you tend to overlook?',model:'I often notice a leader’s stated goal before asking who bears its cost. I can make that second question part of evaluating the proposal.',evidence:['Samuel describes resources a king will take.','The people continue to request a king after the warning.','Every narrated ruler is an example to imitate.','The warning evaluates only the king’s popularity.'],claim:'Political promises must be evaluated alongside the burdens imposed on others.',application:'Ask who bears the costs of a leadership decision and how those people can be heard.',decisions:[['A leader’s supporters cite success as enough justification. Which question adds rigor?','Who supplied the resources and bore the losses?','Was the leader admired by our own group?','The warning makes the costs of power visible, including costs to less powerful people.'],['Someone uses this passage to settle every modern constitutional debate. What limit matters?','A reasoned analogy must account for different institutions.','Ancient and modern offices are automatically identical.','The passage informs evaluation without supplying a complete modern political blueprint.']]});

add({unit:5,id:'exile-lament',title:'Exile: history heard alongside grief',reading:'Psalm 137:1–6',ref:[19,137,1,6],objective:'Read communal lament as evidence of displacement without turning it into a universal diagnosis.',body:[
'The psalm remembers weeping away from Zion. Place and memory matter: exile is not simply movement on a map. It can involve the loss of worship, belonging, familiar institutions, and a way of locating oneself within a shared story.',
'The demand for songs places the displaced community’s grief under pressure from others. The psalm asks how a sacred song can be sung in a foreign setting. Read that question as part of the emotional and communal situation, not as a rule forbidding worship outside one location.',
'The pledge to remember Jerusalem gives language to loyalty and loss. Reading it beside the destruction narrated in 2 Kings 25 adds a voice that a chronology alone cannot supply. Narrative and poetry offer different kinds of evidence about a catastrophe.',
'The full psalm ends with disturbing vengeance language. Do not hide it or treat every cry of pain as an ethical instruction. Lament can make anger speakable while Christian application remains accountable to Jesus’ teaching and the dignity of other people.'
],simple:'A timeline records displacement; a lament voices what it feels like. Hearing grief does not require endorsing every action grief imagines.',vocab:[['Exile','Displacement from a homeland or familiar community.'],['Lament','Speech expressing grief, protest, or loss.'],['Zion','A name associated with Jerusalem and its religious significance.']],deeper:'Read the whole psalm in Explore. Distinguish the expression of pain from a command to imitate violence; do not remove the difficult ending from your interpretation.',reflect:'How could you acknowledge someone’s loss without demanding a quick hopeful ending?',model:'I can listen without asking the person to make the story uplifting for me. I can acknowledge anger while declining to justify harm.',evidence:['The speakers remember Zion with tears.','The demand for songs occurs within displacement.','Grief becomes legitimate only after it sounds optimistic.','Every imagined act in a lament becomes a command for readers.'],claim:'Displacement includes losses that communal lament makes audible.',application:'Make room for the account of loss before offering explanations or demanding optimism.',decisions:[['Someone asks a grieving person to tell only the positive side. What response fits?','Allow grief to be spoken without requiring an uplifting performance.','Insist that lament undermines hope.','The psalm gives loss and protest a place within religious speech.'],['A reader wants to imitate the full psalm’s vengeance. What distinction is needed?','Voicing anger is different from authorizing an action.','Every first-person desire is a moral command.','Genre and the wider ethical conversation matter when moving from expression to conduct.']]});

add({unit:5,id:'return-rebuilding',title:'Return: hope becomes the work of rebuilding',reading:'Ezra 1:1–6',ref:[15,1,1,6],objective:'Trace how permission, participation, and material support shape a return narrative.',body:[
'Ezra opens with a proclamation allowing the rebuilding of the temple in Jerusalem. The story places political action within its account of God’s purposes. It does not require readers to regard every action of the ruler as morally approved.',
'The return involves people responding and neighbors supplying practical resources. Hope becomes a journey and a building task. A decree can open a possibility without completing the human work that follows from it.',
'Return should not be confused with a simple restoration of everything lost. Read further in Ezra to encounter conflict and questions about identity and belonging. This opening is the beginning of a difficult process, not a guarantee that a community’s future will be uncomplicated.',
'A contemporary application can distinguish an opportunity from its fulfillment. When rebuilding after disruption, people need resources, participation, and attention to those affected. The analogy is useful without turning a personal recovery story into the exact fulfillment of this ancient proclamation.'
],simple:'A door opens, people respond, and others supply resources. Rebuilding still requires shared work.',vocab:[['Restoration','Repair or renewal after loss; it need not recreate the past exactly.'],['Temple','The central sanctuary in Jerusalem in these narratives.'],['Participation','Taking an active part in a shared undertaking.']],deeper:'Ask what the opening scene establishes and what only later chapters can show. Hope at the start of a project is not evidence that every later decision was right.',reflect:'What practical support turns a hopeful announcement into a possible action?',model:'An invitation to rebuild needs time, materials, and people who can participate. I would ask what is missing before celebrating the announcement as completion.',evidence:['The proclamation permits rebuilding in Jerusalem.','People respond and receive material support.','The opening proves no conflict will occur later.','Permission alone completes the rebuilding.'],claim:'An opportunity for restoration becomes meaningful through participation and resources.',application:'Identify the practical support needed to turn a hopeful proposal into responsible action.',decisions:[['A team celebrates an announcement as a completed recovery. What is missing?','The work and resources required to carry it out.','A more triumphant description of the announcement.','Ezra distinguishes the opening of a possibility from the participation that follows.'],['The rebuilding faces conflict. Must the original hope have been meaningless?','No; assess the conflict and the needed response.','Yes; genuine hope guarantees a frictionless process.','An opening scene does not promise that later decisions or circumstances will be simple.']],sequence:['Follow the opening movement of Ezra.',['A proclamation permits return and rebuilding.','People prepare to take part.','Neighbors provide practical support.'],'Distinguish the permission, the response, and the support.']});

add({unit:6,id:'psalms-lament',title:'Psalms: learning to pray without pretending',reading:'Psalm 13:1–6',ref:[19,13,1,6],objective:'Trace lament’s movement without making it a required emotional timetable.',body:[
'Psalm 13 begins by asking how long the speaker must endure apparent abandonment. The prayer does not conceal its distress. A beginner need not manufacture certainty before encountering this kind of biblical language; the question itself is addressed to God.',
'The middle of the psalm asks for attention and help. Lament can involve a specific appeal rather than only a general statement that life is difficult. Naming what is feared or needed gives prayer a concrete relationship with the speaker’s circumstances.',
'The ending turns toward trust and song. Read that movement without assuming every person must reach the same emotional place after six verses or six minutes. The literary shape offers language for prayer; it does not set a deadline for another person’s grief.',
'Practice by writing an address, an honest concern, and a request. A statement of hope may follow if it can be spoken honestly. Prayer can accompany practical support and professional care; it should not become pressure to hide symptoms or stop seeking help.'
],simple:'Honest distress can be part of prayer. A psalm’s movement is an invitation, not a stopwatch for healing.',vocab:[['Petition','A request made in prayer.'],['Trust','Reliance that can coexist with unanswered questions.'],['Address','Speech directed to someone, here to God.']],deeper:'Compare different psalms before deciding all prayer must follow one emotional pattern. An individual poem’s ending does not invalidate a person who remains distressed.',reflect:'What honest request could you put into words without forcing a resolution?',model:'I could ask for enough support to face the next day. That request leaves my situation open while refusing to hide it.',evidence:['The psalm begins with questions about apparent absence.','The speaker asks for attention before the ending’s turn toward trust.','The psalm requires every grieving person to recover on its timetable.','An unanswered question makes prayer impossible.'],claim:'Prayer can contain distress, a request, and trust without erasing the distress.',application:'Give a person room for honest prayer without imposing a deadline for recovery.',decisions:[['A friend cannot yet say the psalm’s final words sincerely. What is appropriate?','Let them pray its questions or remain quietly present.','Tell them their prayer fails unless they feel resolved.','The poem provides language; it does not grade a person’s emotional progress.'],['Someone proposes prayer instead of all practical help. What is a fuller response?','Offer prayer alongside concrete support.','Assume practical help demonstrates a lack of trust.','Naming need can invite responsible action rather than make action unnecessary.']],sequence:['Rebuild the movement of this psalm without treating it as a timetable for everyone.',['Questions name distress.','A request asks God to attend and help.','The speaker voices trust and song.'],'Follow the order in this particular poem.']});

add({unit:6,id:'proverbs-discernment',title:'Proverbs: wisdom is more than a slogan',reading:'Proverbs 26:4–5',ref:[20,26,4,5],objective:'Explain why contrasting proverbs call for judgment about circumstances.',body:[
'These adjacent sayings give different directions about answering a fool. Reading only one as an absolute rule misses the deliberate pressure created by their placement together. The reader has to ask what danger each saying addresses.',
'One warns against becoming like the person being answered. The other warns against allowing that person to remain wise in their own eyes. Both are concerned with the effect of a response, but they highlight different possible effects.',
'Wisdom therefore requires attention to audience, purpose, and likely consequences. Silence might avoid an unproductive quarrel; a calm correction might protect people who would otherwise be misled. Neither move is automatically right in every situation.',
'The term fool in wisdom writing should not become an excuse to dismiss anyone who disagrees. Test the claim and the proposed response. A wise reply can acknowledge uncertainty, correct a false statement, and refuse the invitation to exchange contempt.'
],simple:'One saying warns about joining a quarrel; the other warns about leaving error unchallenged. Choose with the situation in view.',vocab:[['Proverb','A compact saying intended to cultivate judgment.'],['Discernment','Careful judgment about a situation and an appropriate response.'],['Consequence','An effect that helps evaluate whether a response is wise.']],deeper:'Do not solve the tension merely by declaring one verse irrelevant. Ask how the two warnings can both inform a decision.',reflect:'When might answering a misleading claim help others, and when might it amplify the quarrel?',model:'I would consider whether a clear correction reaches people being misled. I can respond to the claim without imitating an insulting tone.',evidence:['One saying warns against becoming like the fool.','The other warns against leaving the fool wise in their own eyes.','Only one of these sayings can ever have value.','Wisdom requires answering every provocation immediately.'],claim:'Wise response considers different risks rather than applying one slogan mechanically.',application:'Decide whether a response will correct harm or merely reproduce the quarrel.',decisions:[['An insult is posted only to provoke a fight. What should you assess first?','Whether engaging would reproduce the behavior being criticized.','Whether silence always means agreement.','Verse 4 makes the danger of becoming like the speaker relevant.'],['A misleading claim is harming people who trust it. What else matters?','Whether a calm correction can prevent that harm.','Whether avoiding every reply is always the wisest policy.','Verse 5 makes the cost of leaving a false confidence unchallenged relevant.']]});

add({unit:6,id:'job-listening',title:'Job and the limits of easy explanations',reading:'Job 2:11–13',ref:[18,2,11,13],objective:'Distinguish compassionate presence from unwarranted explanations of suffering.',body:[
'Job’s friends come after hearing of his suffering. Their first response includes grief, physical presence, and silence. The scene recognizes that another person’s pain may be too great for a quick explanation or a polished religious answer.',
'The friends’ later speeches complicate this beginning. Read the complete book before treating any speaker’s confident statement as its final teaching. A dialogue can include a view in order to expose its limitations; quotation alone does not establish endorsement.',
'Job’s story challenges easy equations between suffering and personal wrongdoing. This does not mean actions never have consequences. It means another person’s suffering is not sufficient evidence to diagnose their guilt or God’s hidden intention.',
'Compassionate presence may include listening, bringing food, or helping arrange support. Silence is useful when it makes room for another person, not when it avoids necessary action. Ask what would help and remain willing to hear a response that differs from your own expectation.'
],simple:'The friends begin by showing up. Understanding a person’s pain matters more than winning an argument about why it happened.',vocab:[['Dialogue','A work presenting multiple voices that must be evaluated in context.'],['Presence','Attentive companionship rather than an attempt to control another’s feelings.'],['Inference','A conclusion drawn from evidence, which may exceed what the evidence supports.']],deeper:'Compare the friends’ opening actions with their later arguments and the book’s evaluation of them. A speaker’s confidence is not a substitute for literary context.',reflect:'What might support look like if you gave up the need to explain the loss?',model:'I could ask whether food, company, or help with an appointment would be useful. I do not need a theory of divine intention to provide those things.',evidence:['The friends come to express sympathy and comfort.','They initially sit in silence because the suffering is great.','Severe suffering is enough evidence to establish the sufferer’s guilt.','Every later speech by a friend is automatically the book’s final verdict.'],claim:'Compassion can begin with presence before explanation.',application:'Ask what support is useful without assigning an unverified cause to another person’s suffering.',decisions:[['A friend has experienced a major loss. What first response fits this scene?','Listen and ask what practical support would help.','Explain the precise reason God must have allowed it.','The opening scene models presence without claiming access to a hidden cause.'],['A reader quotes one friend as the book’s final teaching. What should you do?','Read the speech within the whole dialogue.','Accept it because any biblical speaker must be correct.','Who speaks and how the work evaluates the speech are part of interpretation.']]});

add({unit:6,id:'wisdom-time-love',title:'Ecclesiastes and Song: limits, delight, and poetry',reading:'Ecclesiastes 3:1–8',ref:[21,3,1,8],objective:'Read wisdom poetry about time and love without turning every image into a universal command.',body:[
'Ecclesiastes arranges contrasting times in pairs. The poem directs attention to the range of human experience and the limits of control. Its list is not a command that every person must initiate every named action.',
'The book asks difficult questions about work, gain, loss, and what endures. Read its observations within the larger inquiry rather than using one line to declare every action pointless. Wisdom can unsettle easy promises of control while still directing attention to the life actually available.',
'Song of Songs gives biblical space to desire, delight, and the voices of lovers. Read Song 2:8–13 in the additional passage. Its springtime imagery works poetically; the reading need not choose between denying embodied affection and turning each flower into a separate secret code.',
'Jewish and Christian readers have also developed allegorical readings of Song. These should be identified as interpretations rather than quietly substituted for the poem’s language. Together, these wisdom books widen an introduction beyond rules and timelines to include limitation, affection, beauty, and attentive reading.'
],simple:'Wisdom poetry can explore what we cannot control and what we can delight in. An image or listed season is not automatically an instruction.',vocab:[['Poetic pairing','Placing contrasting images or actions together to develop a concern.'],['Allegorical reading','An interpretation that relates a text’s persons and images to another level of meaning.'],['Embodiment','Human life experienced through bodies and material relationships.']],deeper:'Compare the poem’s imagery with what an interpreter says it signifies. A later spiritual reading should explain its relationship to the words actually present.',reflect:'What changes when the Bible is allowed to include delight as well as instruction?',model:'I can read affection and beauty as subjects worth attention instead of insisting that every poem must first become a rule or timeline.',evidence:['Ecclesiastes pairs contrasting times and actions.','The poem includes experiences people cannot simply control.','Every listed action is a universal command to perform it now.','A poetic image has value only if decoded as a secret rule.'],claim:'Wisdom poetry explores the range and limits of human experience.',application:'Distinguish a poem’s depiction of experience from a universal command or an unsupported symbolic code.',decisions:[['A reader treats a listed time as permission for any action they prefer. What is missing?','The poem’s form and purpose within the wider inquiry.','A stronger claim that the list cancels ethical judgment.','A poetic list does not authorize every action in every circumstance.'],['Someone insists Song cannot concern human affection at all. What should they read first?','The lovers’ voices and imagery before proposing another interpretive level.','Only a later explanation without examining the poem.','An interpretation should account for the primary text rather than erase it.']],extraReadings:[{reading:'Song of Songs 2:8–13',ref:[22,2,8,13]}]});

add({unit:7,id:'amos-worship',title:'Amos: when worship and conduct collide',reading:'Amos 5:21–24',ref:[30,5,21,24],objective:'Explain the force of prophetic criticism without concluding all worship is worthless.',body:[
'Amos uses an uncompromising rejection of festivals, offerings, and songs. The shock matters: activities associated with religious devotion are not accepted as substitutes for justice. The passage challenges a separation between public worship and the way a community treats people.',
'The contrast culminates in flowing water imagery for justice and righteousness. Read the image as a demand for sustained communal conduct, not merely an attractive line about nature. The rhetorical force depends on what the passage places against the religious activity.',
'The critique does not require the claim that singing or ritual is always meaningless. Its target is worship severed from the concern expressed in the final demand. An interpretation should account for that contrast instead of selecting either the ritual rejection or the water image alone.',
'A contemporary community can examine whether its celebrations conceal unfair practices. Ask about access, treatment of workers, use of money, and attention to those who are harmed. The prophetic text gives the examination moral urgency; specific accusations still require evidence.'
],simple:'Religious activity cannot substitute for treating people justly. The point is to reconnect worship and conduct.',vocab:[['Prophetic critique','A challenge to conduct voiced within a message about God’s purposes.'],['Justice','Right dealing with others, especially where power affects their treatment.'],['Rhetoric','The way language is shaped to persuade or confront.']],deeper:'Ask what the contrast accomplishes. A shocking rejection within a specific argument is not automatically a prohibition of every form of worship.',reflect:'What might a community celebrate while failing to examine its conduct?',model:'A successful gathering might coexist with unfair treatment of its workers. I would investigate that treatment rather than let attendance numbers settle the question.',evidence:['The passage rejects religious activities within its critique.','Its closing demand concerns justice and righteousness.','High attendance alone proves a community’s conduct is just.','The passage’s only concern is the musical quality of worship.'],claim:'Religious performance cannot replace justice in communal conduct.',application:'Examine a concrete practice instead of treating visible religious success as sufficient evidence of faithfulness.',decisions:[['A congregation points to a crowded festival when unfair treatment is reported. What should happen?','Investigate the treatment itself.','Treat attendance as proof the report must be false.','Amos refuses to let religious activity substitute for examining conduct.'],['Someone concludes that every song is forbidden. What is missing?','The contrast between ritual activity and justice.','An even broader claim that all communal practices are useless.','The passage’s argument concerns the relation between worship and conduct.']]});

add({unit:7,id:'micah-mercy',title:'Micah: justice, mercy, and humility',reading:'Micah 6:6–8',ref:[33,6,6,8],objective:'Distinguish escalating religious offerings from the ethical response the passage names.',body:[
'The questions begin with what someone might bring before God and escalate toward increasingly extreme offerings. Read the sequence carefully: the rhetorical questions create a problem, and the response redirects attention. Not every possibility named in a question is endorsed.',
'The answer names doing justice, loving mercy, and walking humbly with God. These are relational actions and dispositions rather than a price list. The passage challenges the assumption that the right quantity of an offering can bypass responsibility for how one lives.',
'Humility matters for the reader as well as for the ancient audience. Applying a prophetic text does not give us infallible knowledge of everyone else’s motives. We need honest evidence, attention to those affected, and willingness to examine our own conduct.',
'This text can guide a practical response without becoming a technique for purchasing salvation. Within this course, grace is God’s gift and ethical action is a response. Keep that distinction while allowing the prophet’s demand to retain its force.'
],simple:'The passage turns from “How much can I offer?” to “How should I live with God and others?”',vocab:[['Mercy','Compassion expressed in the treatment of others.'],['Humility','Recognition of one’s limits and accountability.'],['Rhetorical question','A question used to develop an argument rather than request a simple factual answer.']],deeper:'Follow the question-and-answer movement. An extreme possibility mentioned in a question must not be mistaken for a practice the passage commands.',reflect:'What action would combine accountability with mercy in a conflict you can imagine?',model:'I could name a harmful action clearly while allowing a path to repair. Mercy would not require pretending no harm occurred.',evidence:['The questions escalate the proposed offerings.','The answer names justice, mercy, and humility.','Every offering mentioned in the questions is endorsed.','A sufficiently expensive gift makes conduct irrelevant.'],claim:'The passage redirects religious bargaining toward responsible relationship.',application:'Choose an action that combines fair dealing, compassion, and willingness to be corrected.',decisions:[['A donor offers money to avoid addressing mistreatment. What does this reading challenge?','Using an offering to bypass changed conduct.','The possibility that material generosity can ever help.','The contrast targets substitution for responsibility, not every use of a material gift.'],['A learner quotes an extreme offering as a divine command. What should they inspect?','Its role in the rhetorical questions and their answer.','Only whether the words appear somewhere in the passage.','Presence in a rhetorical question is not endorsement.']]});

add({unit:7,id:'jeremiah-hope',title:'Jeremiah: hope addressed to a displaced community',reading:'Jeremiah 29:4–14',ref:[24,29,4,14],objective:'Read a familiar promise within its communal audience and extended time horizon.',body:[
'Jeremiah’s letter addresses people living in exile. Instructions to build, plant, form households, and seek the city’s welfare concern a sustained life in displacement. The audience is not told that faith will make the situation disappear immediately.',
'The warning about misleading voices matters alongside the promises. Not every reassuring message is trustworthy merely because it sounds religious. The letter directs the community toward practices that can continue while they wait.',
'The promise of a future and hope belongs within this address and its stated horizon. It is not a personal guarantee of instant financial success or a preferred career. Reading the promise in context does not remove hope; it changes the kind of hope being offered.',
'A contemporary reader can learn to combine longing with responsible life in present circumstances. Work for the welfare of neighbors, assess comforting claims carefully, and avoid treating a delayed outcome as proof that a person lacks faith.'
],simple:'This hope accompanies a community through waiting. It does not promise that faithful people immediately get their preferred circumstances.',vocab:[['Audience','The people addressed by a passage.'],['Time horizon','The period within which a text places an expectation.'],['Communal hope','Hope concerning the life of a people, not only an individual outcome.']],deeper:'Compare the instructions, warning, and promise before applying a single verse. Which parts would be missed if the promise were printed by itself?',reflect:'What responsible action can coexist with waiting for circumstances to change?',model:'I can invest in the welfare of people around me while hoping for a different future. Waiting does not have to mean abandoning present responsibilities.',evidence:['The letter instructs exiles to build and plant.','It calls for concern for the city where they live.','The promise guarantees each reader an immediate preferred career.','Present responsibilities are suspended until hope is fulfilled.'],claim:'Hope in this letter supports sustained communal life during displacement.',application:'Pair a long-term hope with one responsible action for present neighbors.',decisions:[['A reader uses the promise to guarantee a promotion next week. What needs checking?','The original audience and stated time horizon.','Whether the reader has repeated the verse often enough.','The letter addresses a displaced community rather than guaranteeing that individual timetable.'],['A group decides waiting means doing nothing. What counters that?','The instructions to build, plant, and seek the city’s welfare.','The claim that hopeful people never need practical plans.','The letter gives waiting a form of responsible activity.']]});

add({unit:8,id:'kingdom-following',title:'God’s kingdom and the call to follow',reading:'Mark 1:14–20',ref:[41,1,14,20],objective:'Connect Jesus’ announcement of God’s reign with repentance and discipleship.',body:[
'Mark introduces Jesus’ proclamation with language of fulfillment and the nearness of God’s kingdom. Kingdom refers here to God’s reign, not simply a location people visit after death. The announcement invites a response in the present.',
'Repentance and belief name that response. Repentance involves a changed direction, not only an unpleasant feeling about oneself. Trust is directed toward the good news; it should not be confused with claiming perfect understanding before beginning.',
'The calling of fishers gives the invitation a narrative form. People are addressed within ordinary work and begin to follow. The later story includes their misunderstanding and failure, so the initial response must not be mistaken for instant maturity.',
'A reader can distinguish the call’s significance from a mechanical instruction that every Christian must leave the same kind of employment. Ask how following Jesus changes priorities, relationships, and conduct in an actual life. The application needs a reasoned connection to the narrative.'
],simple:'Jesus announces God’s reign and invites a changed direction of life. Following is the beginning of learning, not its completion.',vocab:[['Kingdom of God','God’s reign or active rule, not merely a geographical destination.'],['Repentance','Turning from sin toward a changed direction of life.'],['Disciple','A learner and follower whose understanding develops.']],deeper:'Compare the disciples’ initial response with later scenes in Mark. Their failures prevent us from equating beginning discipleship with already understanding everything.',reflect:'What is the difference between feeling regret and changing direction?',model:'Regret names how I feel; changing direction could include admitting harm and altering a repeated behavior. The two can be related without being identical.',evidence:['Jesus’ announcement calls for repentance and belief.','The calling scene takes place within ordinary work.','Following begins only after someone understands everything.','Kingdom means only a place after death, with no present significance.'],claim:'The announcement of God’s reign invites a present response and an ongoing life of learning.',application:'Identify a concrete change in conduct that gives a stated priority practical form.',decisions:[['A beginner feels unready because many questions remain. What does the disciples’ story allow?','A meaningful beginning followed by continued learning.','A requirement to settle every doctrine before learning can start.','The disciples’ initial response precedes much misunderstanding and growth.'],['Someone says every believer must abandon their occupation immediately. What distinction helps?','Separate this calling scene from a universal occupational rule.','Treat each narrated detail as an identical command to all readers.','Narrative application needs attention to audience, role, and circumstance.']],sequence:['Reconstruct Mark’s opening movement in this passage.',['Jesus announces God’s kingdom.','The announcement calls for repentance and belief.','Jesus calls people within their work.','They begin to follow.'],'Follow proclamation, response, and the narrated example of calling.']});

add({unit:8,id:'parables-welcome',title:'Parables: hearing the question behind the story',reading:'Luke 15:1–10',ref:[42,15,1,10],objective:'Interpret parables through their surrounding conversation and central comparison.',body:[
'The setting includes people approaching Jesus and a complaint about his welcome and shared meals. The stories answer that situation. Reading only an image of a sheep or coin can miss the question the story is addressing.',
'The searches and celebrations make finding the lost a reason for joy. The repeated movement strengthens the comparison. Readers should ask how that joy challenges the complaint rather than assigning a separate hidden doctrine to every incidental object.',
'A parable can invite identification and then unsettle it. Someone who initially identifies with a responsible keeper may need to consider their response to the recovery of someone they had dismissed. The story works through imagination as well as direct instruction.',
'The course’s practice of welcome can draw on this challenge while distinguishing the text’s setting from every modern admission debate. Ask whether a community celebrates restoration or resents attention given to those outside its favored circle. A responsible application names both the connection and its limits.'
],simple:'The stories answer a complaint about welcome. Watch what is found and who is invited to rejoice.',vocab:[['Parable','A story or comparison that invites insight through a familiar situation.'],['Literary setting','The surrounding material that shapes a passage’s meaning.'],['Central comparison','The main relationship a story uses to illuminate its concern.']],deeper:'Not every detail must stand for a separate doctrine. Test an interpretation by whether it explains the complaint, the search, and the celebration together.',reflect:'When might a community resent someone else receiving attention or a second chance?',model:'A group may welcome people in principle but resent the time their needs require. I would ask whether its actual response shares the story’s joy.',evidence:['The stories follow a complaint about Jesus welcoming people.','Finding what was lost leads to celebration.','Each incidental detail must encode a separate doctrine.','The surrounding complaint is irrelevant to interpretation.'],claim:'The stories challenge resentment of welcome by inviting joy in recovery.',application:'Examine whether a community’s response to someone’s return makes its welcome credible.',decisions:[['A reader starts decoding the coin’s material into a doctrine. What should come first?','Examine the complaint and repeated search-and-celebration pattern.','Assume every object has an independent secret meaning.','The surrounding conversation and central comparison constrain interpretation.'],['A group says welcome is fine as long as nobody needs attention. What does the story press?','Whether their response can include joy and practical attention.','Whether welcome can remain a slogan with no consequences.','The parables make recovery a reason for response rather than detached approval.']]});

add({unit:8,id:'gospel-comparison',title:'Four Gospels: compare without flattening',reading:'Luke 1:1–4',ref:[42,1,1,4],objective:'Explain how a Gospel’s purpose and arrangement shape its presentation of Jesus.',body:[
'Luke’s opening describes earlier accounts, transmitted testimony, investigation, and an ordered presentation. The author states a purpose for writing. This gives beginners a way to ask how a Gospel communicates rather than treating it as a contextless collection of sayings.',
'The Gospels share a central subject while arranging material and emphasizing concerns differently. Read a scene within one account before combining it with another. A combined retelling may be useful, but it can hide the reason a particular writer places a scene where they do.',
'Comparison should record agreement and difference before trying to explain either. Neither immediate harmonization nor an immediate charge of fraud is a substitute for close reading. Distinguish a literary observation from a historical conclusion that would require further evidence.',
'Use Explore to compare the openings of Mark and Luke. Record each opening’s starting point, announced concerns, and effect on a new reader. Then identify one question the comparison raises. The goal is a more attentive reading of both accounts, not a contest in which one must be discarded.'
],simple:'Read each Gospel as a shaped account. Compare what each writer emphasizes before combining their stories.',vocab:[['Gospel','A written account proclaiming the good news concerning Jesus.'],['Arrangement','The order and placement through which an author presents material.'],['Harmonization','Combining accounts into a single reconstruction, which may obscure distinctive emphases.']],deeper:'Luke’s claim to investigation is part of the primary text. Evaluating a particular historical question requires examining relevant evidence beyond simply repeating that claim.',reflect:'What could be lost when two accounts are immediately merged?',model:'I might overlook why one author begins with testimony and another with proclamation. I can describe those choices before proposing an explanation.',evidence:['Luke’s opening mentions earlier accounts and testimony.','The author identifies a purpose for an ordered account.','Every difference in arrangement proves deception.','A combined retelling always preserves every author’s emphasis.'],claim:'An author’s purpose and arrangement shape how an account should be read.',application:'Compare two openings on their own terms before constructing a combined summary.',decisions:[['Two Gospel passages arrange material differently. What is the first analytical step?','Record the difference and examine each literary setting.','Declare a motive before reading either context.','Observation should precede an explanation of why the difference exists.'],['A combined summary leaves out Luke’s stated purpose. What should be restored?','The author’s own account of why the work was written.','Only the details that fit the combined summary most easily.','A useful comparison preserves distinctive concerns rather than treating them as clutter.']]});
})();
